# Create Analyze Rule

Use when analyzing code quality, fixing lint/analyzer issues, or preparing code for commit.

## Commands

### Static Analysis

```bash
dart analyze
# or
flutter analyze
```

Finds: type errors, unused code, style issues, null safety problems.

### Format Code

```bash
dart format .
```

Formats all Dart files. Use before commit.

### Format Check (CI)

```bash
dart format --set-exit-if-changed .
```

Exits non-zero if any file needs formatting. Use in CI.

### Build Runner (after DI/i18n changes)

```bash
dart run build_runner build --delete-conflicting-outputs
```

Regenerates: injection.config.dart, resources.g.dart, etc.

### Pub Get

```bash
flutter pub get
```

After pubspec.yaml changes.

## Typical Workflow

1. Make code changes
2. `dart analyze` - fix any issues
3. `dart format .` - format code
4. If DI or i18n changed: `dart run build_runner build --delete-conflicting-outputs`
5. `flutter pub get` if dependencies changed

## Using Dart MCP Server

When the Dart/Flutter MCP server is enabled (via plugin .mcp.json), the AI can:

- Run `dart analyze` and interpret results
- Apply fixes for analyzer errors
- Format code
- Search pub.dev for packages
- Run tests

Prefer MCP tools over manual shell when available.

## Common Analyzer Fixes

- Add missing imports
- Use `?` for nullable types
- Add `const` where possible
- Fix `copyWith` null handling: `status ?? this.status`
- Remove unused variables/imports

## Checklist

- [ ] dart analyze passes with no errors
- [ ] dart format applied
- [ ] build_runner run if DI/i18n changed
- [ ] No debug print in production paths (or use proper logging)
